WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.058 --> 00:00:03.193
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:03.437 --> 00:00:07.256
In today's T-Level Chemistry lesson, we are going to study

3
00:00:07.372 --> 00:00:11.006
A twelve point four, Catalysts in Industrial

4
00:00:11.076 --> 00:00:12.016
Reactions.

5
00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:16.161
By the end of this lesson, you should be able to explain

6
00:00:16.254 --> 00:00:19.934
how a catalyst increases industrial rate, how that

7
00:00:19.992 --> 00:00:23.742
shortens turnaround time and can reduce costs, and why

8
00:00:23.824 --> 00:00:25.646
delta H does not change.

9
00:00:26.041 --> 00:00:30.406
A catalyst increases reaction rate without being permanently

10
00:00:30.476 --> 00:00:32.450
chemically changed overall.

11
00:00:32.787 --> 00:00:36.826
It provides an alternative pathway with lower activation

12
00:00:36.943 --> 00:00:37.511
energy.

13
00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:41.447
Activation energy is the minimum energy required for a

14
00:00:41.563 --> 00:00:42.864
reaction to occur.

15
00:00:43.258 --> 00:00:46.869
Specification point A twelve point four point

16
00:00:46.985 --> 00:00:51.188
one links increased rate to faster turnaround and reduced

17
00:00:51.258 --> 00:00:51.815
costs.

18
00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.879
The industrial chain is: catalyst, increased reaction

19
00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.571
rate, shorter reaction stage, faster turnaround.

20
00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:03.797
That can allow more production cycles in the same period,

21
00:01:03.971 --> 00:01:08.243
which can reduce operating costs or increase capacity.

22
00:01:08.951 --> 00:01:12.713
Do not claim that every catalyst makes every part of a process

23
00:01:12.771 --> 00:01:13.294
cheaper.

24
00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:17.415
Real cost still includes catalyst purchase, recovery or

25
00:01:17.485 --> 00:01:21.282
replacement, energy, maintenance and separation.

26
00:01:21.618 --> 00:01:23.395
Case study from the lesson.

27
00:01:23.731 --> 00:01:27.585
Without a catalyst the reaction stage takes eight point zero

28
00:01:27.655 --> 00:01:28.166
hours.

29
00:01:28.282 --> 00:01:31.510
With a catalyst it takes three point zero hours.

30
00:01:31.788 --> 00:01:34.505
The reduction is five point zero hours.

31
00:01:34.784 --> 00:01:38.940
The percentage time saved is eight minus three, divided

32
00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:42.841
by eight, times one hundred, which is sixty two point

33
00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:45.789
five percent less time on the reaction stage.

34
00:01:46.242 --> 00:01:50.027
An examination example uses ten hours reduced

35
00:01:50.062 --> 00:01:51.060
to four hours.

36
00:01:51.397 --> 00:01:53.812
The time reduction is six hours.

37
00:01:54.427 --> 00:01:58.502
Process X runs twelve batches a week without the catalyst

38
00:01:58.677 --> 00:02:01.021
and eighteen batches a week with it.

39
00:02:01.358 --> 00:02:03.587
That is six extra batches each week.

40
00:02:03.866 --> 00:02:07.802
The reaction-stage cost falls from nine hundred pounds to seven

41
00:02:07.871 --> 00:02:11.668
hundred pounds per batch, a saving of two hundred pounds per

42
00:02:11.726 --> 00:02:12.190
batch.

43
00:02:12.643 --> 00:02:16.614
Specification point A twelve point four point two:

44
00:02:16.823 --> 00:02:19.458
catalysts reduce activation energy.

45
00:02:19.702 --> 00:02:21.397
Watch the energy profile.

46
00:02:21.606 --> 00:02:23.858
Reactant enthalpy is the same.

47
00:02:24.009 --> 00:02:26.029
Product enthalpy is the same.

48
00:02:26.273 --> 00:02:28.804
The uncatalysed peak is higher.

49
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.987
The catalysed peak is lower.

50
00:02:31.231 --> 00:02:34.365
Delta H is the same on both pathways.

51
00:02:34.539 --> 00:02:38.777
At the same temperature, the lower barrier means a greater proportion

52
00:02:38.846 --> 00:02:40.472
of collisions can react.

53
00:02:40.867 --> 00:02:44.024
Successful collisions occur more frequently.

54
00:02:44.361 --> 00:02:48.378
The catalyst does not give particles extra kinetic

55
00:02:48.494 --> 00:02:49.110
energy.

56
00:02:49.353 --> 00:02:51.455
Correct the misconceptions.

57
00:02:51.733 --> 00:02:54.160
A catalyst does not heat the mixture.

58
00:02:54.299 --> 00:02:56.099
It does not change delta H.

59
00:02:56.366 --> 00:02:58.246
It is not consumed overall.

60
00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:01.729
And a faster reaction does not mean zero cost.

61
00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:03.970
Examination-style question.

62
00:03:04.469 --> 00:03:05.329
Part a.

63
00:03:05.654 --> 00:03:09.857
Give one industrial advantage of increasing rate using a catalyst.

64
00:03:09.949 --> 00:03:10.762
Three marks.

65
00:03:10.936 --> 00:03:11.993
Rate increases.

66
00:03:12.109 --> 00:03:15.685
The stage completes sooner, so turnaround is faster.

67
00:03:15.859 --> 00:03:20.120
Costs can fall, or more production cycles can be completed.

68
00:03:20.398 --> 00:03:21.165
Part b.

69
00:03:21.374 --> 00:03:25.681
Explain how a catalyst increases rate in terms of activation

70
00:03:25.774 --> 00:03:26.424
energy.

71
00:03:26.552 --> 00:03:27.399
Three marks.

72
00:03:27.515 --> 00:03:29.617
It provides an alternative pathway.

73
00:03:29.826 --> 00:03:31.788
Activation energy is lower.

74
00:03:32.031 --> 00:03:36.060
More collisions overcome the barrier, so more successful collisions

75
00:03:36.141 --> 00:03:37.720
occur per unit time.

76
00:03:37.999 --> 00:03:38.881
Part c.

77
00:03:39.125 --> 00:03:42.144
A stage falls from ten hours to four hours.

78
00:03:42.353 --> 00:03:43.769
State the time reduction.

79
00:03:43.816 --> 00:03:44.489
One mark.

80
00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:45.476
Six hours.

81
00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:46.347
Part d.

82
00:03:46.799 --> 00:03:49.377
Why does a catalyst not change delta H?

83
00:03:49.528 --> 00:03:50.352
Two marks.

84
00:03:50.631 --> 00:03:53.429
Reactant enthalpy is unchanged.

85
00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.272
Product enthalpy is unchanged, so delta H is

86
00:03:57.364 --> 00:03:58.119
unchanged.

87
00:03:58.514 --> 00:04:00.790
Before we finish, complete the model.

88
00:04:00.906 --> 00:04:04.052
A catalyst lowers E a and increases rate.

89
00:04:04.447 --> 00:04:08.545
Faster rate shortens the reaction stage and can reduce costs

90
00:04:08.882 --> 00:04:10.554
through faster turnaround.

91
00:04:11.169 --> 00:04:14.780
Delta H stays the same because the enthalpy levels

92
00:04:14.838 --> 00:04:17.438
of reactants and products do not change.

93
